---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Icon from '../../components/Icon.astro';
---
<Base title="Current law in India" description="What Indian law actually says about e-cigarettes and smokeless nicotine products, as of today." wide={true}>
  <section class="breakout law-hero">
    <div class="breakout-inner">
      <p class="eyebrow">As of today</p>
      <h1>The law in India, as it stands</h1>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="law-body">
    <div class="status-grid">
      <div class="status-card status-banned">
        <Icon name="shield" />
        <h3>E-cigarettes &amp; ENDS</h3>
        <p class="status-tag">Banned nationally</p>
        <p>Prohibited outright under PECA, 2019 — production, sale, import, advertisement, and (per a 2023 clarification) possession in any quantity.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="status-card status-blocked">
        <Icon name="leaf" />
        <h3>Nicotine pouches &amp; snus</h3>
        <p class="status-tag">No lawful pathway</p>
        <p>Not named in any ban, but blocked by two separate mechanisms — no FSSAI approval to sell, and import treated as case-by-case discretionary refusal.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="status-card status-legal">
        <Icon name="heart" />
        <h3>NRT (gums, patches)</h3>
        <p class="status-tag">Legal &amp; regulated</p>
        <p>Regulated as medicines under the Drugs and Cosmetics Act, 1940, and actively supported by government as a cessation aid.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="law-prose">
      <h2><Icon name="shield" /> E-cigarettes and ENDS</h2>
      <p>
        Electronic cigarettes are banned in India under the <a href="https://www.indiacode.nic.in/handle/123456789/13078" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Prohibition of Electronic Cigarettes Act, 2019 (PECA)</a>,
        Act No. 42 of 2019, passed by Parliament on 5 December 2019 (it repealed
        an earlier ordinance of the same name). The Act prohibits the
        production, manufacture, import, export, transport, sale, distribution,
        storage and advertisement of e-cigarettes and all Electronic Nicotine
        Delivery Systems (ENDS), including heat-not-burn products and e-hookah.
      </p>
      <p>
        PECA does not explicitly criminalise personal possession, but the Union
        Health Ministry clarified in October 2023 that possession of
        e-cigarettes "in any quantity" is not possible without contravening the
        Act, given the breadth of the storage and transport prohibitions.
        First-offence penalties include imprisonment of up to one year and a
        fine of up to ₹1 lakh; repeat offences carry higher penalties.
      </p>

      <h2><Icon name="leaf" /> Nicotine pouches and snus</h2>
      <p>
        Nicotine pouches and snus fall outside PECA's definition of ENDS — they
        involve no vapour, aerosol or heating element. That doesn't mean they're
        lawfully available, though — they're blocked by a combination of two
        separate mechanisms, neither of which is a clean, single-line ban the
        way PECA is for e-cigarettes:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>No domestic sale pathway.</strong> As oral products intended
          for human consumption, they fall within the Food Safety and Standards
          Authority of India's (FSSAI) regulatory ambit under the Food Safety
          and Standards Act, 2006, and FSSAI has not approved nicotine for use
          in any food product.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Import is a case-by-case discretionary call, not an outright
          ban.</strong> Nicotine pouches fall under a "restricted" (not
          prohibited) goods classification with India's customs authorities,
          meaning import requires a specific licence for which the Directorate
          General of Foreign Trade (DGFT) consults the health ministry — in
          practice resulting in refusal, but this is administrative discretion
          applied case by case, not a standing statutory prohibition the way
          PECA is.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The net effect — no lawful way to sell, and no reliable way to import —
        is similar to PECA's outcome for e-cigarettes, but the legal <em>mechanism</em>
        genuinely differs, and unlike PECA there is no single Act that names
        nicotine pouches directly. COTPA (2003) doesn't list them among
        regulated tobacco products, PECA covers only electronic/vapour devices,
        and their status under the Drugs and Cosmetics Act, 1940 is unsettled. <a href="https://vajiramandravi.com/current-affairs/nicotine-pouches-in-india-legal-status-regulation-and-health-concerns/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Some commentators have argued</a> a direct Customs Act / Foreign Trade Act notification could close this
        gap cleanly in a way that hasn't yet been done.
      </p>
      <p class="callout-note">
        This is AVI's understanding of the regulatory position, not formal
        legal advice; independent verification is recommended for anyone
        making decisions based on it.
      </p>

      <h2><Icon name="heart" /> Nicotine replacement therapy (NRT), for contrast</h2>
      <p>
        Gums, patches and similar nicotine replacement products are legal in
        India, regulated as medicines under the Drugs and Cosmetics Act, 1940,
        and receive government support as cessation aids. AVI's position isn't
        that NRT should be restricted — it's that reduced-risk nicotine
        products are being held to a different, stricter standard than NRT
        despite comparable or better real-world quit outcomes; see our <a href="/science">evidence library</a> for the research behind that
        claim.
      </p>

      <h2><Icon name="document" /> What this means practically</h2>
      <p>
        This site does not sell, advertise, or facilitate access to any
        nicotine product, in India or otherwise, and does not provide
        purchasing guidance. Nothing on this site should be read as
        encouraging violation of PECA or any other applicable law. AVI's
        position is that adult smokers should have <em>legal, regulated</em>
        access to lower-risk alternatives — our advocacy is directed at
        changing the law through evidence, litigation and public debate, not
        at circumventing it.
      </p>

      <p>
        See our <a href="/litigation">litigation</a> page for the legal
        challenges AVI has organised and fought against state-level bans that
        preceded PECA, and against the aviation-security flight ban that PECA
        doesn't touch — including the case AVI is actively running today — and
        their current status.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <style>
    .eyebrow {
      font-size: 0.82rem;
      font-weight: 600;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      letter-spacing: 0.08em;
      color: var(--gold-light);
      margin: 0 0 0.75rem;
    }
    .law-hero {
      background: linear-gradient(rgba(18,19,15,0.85), rgba(18,19,15,0.93)), url('/images/backgrounds/open-sky.jpg');
      background-size: cover;
      background-position: center 60%;
      padding: 3.25rem 0 2.75rem;
    }
    .law-hero h1 { color: #fff; margin: 0; font-size: clamp(1.9rem, 3.6vw, 2.6rem); }

    .law-body { max-width: var(--max-w-wide); margin: 0 auto; padding: 2.75rem 1.5rem 4rem; }

    .status-grid {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
      gap: 1.5rem;
      margin-bottom: 3rem;
    }
    .status-card {
      background: #fff;
      border-radius: 8px;
      padding: 1.5rem 1.5rem 1.6rem;
      border-top: 4px solid;
      box-shadow: 0 1px 3px rgba(20,25,20,0.06);
    }
    .status-card :global(svg) { width: 1.8rem; height: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 0.85rem; }
    .status-card h3 { margin: 0 0 0.35rem; font-size: 1.05rem; }
    .status-card p { margin: 0; font-size: 0.88rem; color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.5; }
    .status-tag {
      font-weight: 700 !important;
      font-size: 0.78rem !important;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      letter-spacing: 0.03em;
      margin-bottom: 0.6rem !important;
    }
    .status-banned { border-color: var(--rust); }
    .status-banned :global(svg) { color: var(--rust); }
    .status-banned .status-tag { color: var(--rust-dark); }
    .status-blocked { border-color: var(--gold); }
    .status-blocked :global(svg) { color: var(--gold); }
    .status-blocked .status-tag { color: var(--gold-dark); }
    .status-legal { border-color: var(--accent); }
    .status-legal :global(svg) { color: var(--accent); }
    .status-legal .status-tag { color: var(--accent-dark); }
    @media (max-width: 780px) {
      .status-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    }

    .law-prose { max-width: var(--max-w); }
    .law-prose h2 {
      display: flex;
      align-items: center;
      gap: 0.6rem;
      font-size: 1.25rem;
      margin: 2.5rem 0 1rem;
    }
    .law-prose h2:first-child { margin-top: 0; }
    .law-prose h2 :global(svg) { width: 1.3rem; height: 1.3rem; color: var(--accent); flex-shrink: 0; }
    .callout-note {
      background: var(--paper-alt);
      border-left: 3px solid var(--gold);
      padding: 1rem 1.25rem;
      font-size: 0.9rem;
      font-style: italic;
      color: var(--muted);
    }
  </style>
</Base>
